  'pl.intro': 'Save what you’ve entered as Plan A or Plan B, change the numbers, then compare. Plans are kept in this browser only.',
  'pl.saveA': 'Save as Plan A', 'pl.saveB': 'Save as Plan B', 'pl.A': 'Plan A', 'pl.B': 'Plan B', 'pl.now': 'Now',
  'pl.r1': 'Invested a month', 'pl.r2': 'Retire at', 'pl.r3': 'Spending a month', 'pl.r6': 'Chance it lasts to their live-to age', 'pl.r7': 'Invest a month so it lasts', 'pl.r8': 'Scenario 1 reached at',
  'pl.load': 'Load', 'pl.note': 'Each plan uses its own live-to age. Amounts follow the “Show amounts as” setting.', 'pl.chance': '{p} (to {L})',
  'm.sum': 'How this is worked out',
  'm.p1': 'Until retirement, the pot grows each month by your before-retirement growth rate, then your monthly investing goes in. From retirement it grows at your after-retirement rate instead. Fees, if you add them, come off the growth each year.',
  'm.p2': 'Every figure is in today’s prices unless you switch to actual future amounts: future amounts are shrunk by inflation so you can compare them with what things cost now. Amounts you type in are always in today’s prices, except your monthly investing, which is a cash amount invested as it stands.',
  'm.p3': 'Your share of a month’s gain is what you put in, divided by what you put in plus what the pot grew by on its own.',
  'm.p4': 'Scenario 3 stops adding money the month Scenario 1 is reached, lets the pot grow on its own, and checks when it gets big enough that your monthly investing would be under the Scenario 2 line. Scenarios 1 and 2 after your retirement age assume you keep investing and take nothing out.',
  'm.p5': 'After retirement, each month you take out your spending minus any other income, plus tax on that if you set it. Your spending stays the same in today’s prices, so it rises in cash terms with inflation every year. Other income set to rise with prices does the same; income set as a fixed amount stops rising once it starts, so it buys a little less each year. Other income bigger than your spending isn’t added to the pot. Cash payouts (dividends and interest) are part of the growth rate, not on top of it.',
  'm.p6': '“On track” means the money lasts up to the age on the “Live to age” slider and leaves at least the amount you want to leave. The “Enough” point is the earliest age you could stop adding money and still be on track.',
  'm.p7': 'With two people, both pots are added together and spending starts when you retire. Your partner keeps investing until their own retirement age, unless you stop adding money early, which stops both. Ages on the page are yours. If you each invest differently, untick “Same growth as yours” and add your partner’s growth rates: the page combines the two into one rate, so the pot at your retirement comes out the same as growing each person’s money at their own rate. After retirement it weights the two rates by how much each of you has. The same goes for ups and downs, if you add your partner’s.',
  'm.p8': 'The market test picks a random growth figure for each year from your growth rate and its ups and downs. It uses the same 1,000 possible futures every time, so results don’t jump about as you type. Your growth rate is treated as the typical yearly growth over the long run, the way long-run returns are usually quoted, so in average years the pot ends up close to steady growth. What pulls it down is a run of bad years, especially early in retirement.',
